<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herman Melville</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a>–<a href="/years/1891">1891</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Herman Melvill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herman Melville was an American writer of the American <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> period, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1819. He went to sea in 1839 and in 1841 sailed on the whaler Acushnet, jumped ship in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>, and drew on his encounters with the islanders for his first books, Typee (1846) and Omoo (1847). Moby-Dick (1851) found no audience, and after publishing short fiction such as Benito Cereno in magazines he turned to poetry and worked as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> customs inspector in New York. Little known when he died in 1891, he was the subject of a revival from the centennial of his birth in 1919, and Moby-Dick came to be considered one of the Great American Novels.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herman-melville.jpg" alt="Herman Melville" width="330" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Oriel Eaton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herman_Melville_by_Joseph_O_Eaton.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harper and Brothers published <b>Herman Melville</b>'s Moby-Dick, a month after its London edition.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-14">14 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
